  "notes": [
    "Every measurement uses the real exported Kev checkpoint. No synthetic model, training, or calibration refit.",
    "The typed configuration sweep uses one score transfer per group and blocking input copies. Later measurements use nonblocking input copies.",
    "The typed sweep reference uses current score collection with historical 8-pair settings. The separate original comparison runs the actual original implementation.",
    "Original-comparison one_transfer_only includes asynchronous input copies, consolidated CPU scores and cloned score storage, with identical input grouping/padding.",
    "The default keeps 8 pairs, exact padding, FP32 weights, SDPA, grouping enabled, and an 8192 padded-token budget. On the typed sample an 8-pair forward never exceeds 3840 tokens, so the 16384 comparison cap and 8192 default produce identical batches.",
    "Compilation is opt-in. Some 64-token variants reached the recompilation limit and timings then include eager fallback; per-variant warning counts are retained.",
    "Latency is synchronized score_rows wall time, including tokenization and CPU scores. Combined-batch latency belongs to every participating request; amortized time is a throughput measure.",
    "API measurements use real in-process HTTP/ASGI requests, excluding sockets, Uvicorn limits, TLS and network. Queue wait and response serialization are included.",
    "Closed-loop API clients immediately replace completed requests. Each configuration makes 120 measured calls after 8 warmup requests.",
    "Calibration uses saved temperature and fixed labels. Accuracy accepts any positive target mass; ECE uses mass at the top prediction. This 1000-row sample is a drift check, not a new generalization result.",
    "The default API collection window is zero; the 2ms and zero-delay queue measurements are separate sweeps.",
    "Compiler on-disk caches remain warm between configurations. Original and typed sweeps use three measured passes; heldout runs use one measured pass after the initial full pass."
